---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import { people } from "../lib/people.js";

const withSrc = people.filter((p) => (p.sources || []).length > 0).length;
const counts = {};
for (const p of people) for (const s of p.sources || []) {
  const k = s.split("—")[0].trim();
  counts[k] = (counts[k] || 0) + 1;
}
const ranked = Object.entries(counts).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
---
<Base
  title="Sources"
  eyebrow="Where the evidence comes from"
  dek="Every citation the archive carries, counted — and, more usefully, a plain statement of which of them are records and which are other people's family trees.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Of the {people.length} published people here, <strong>{withSrc}</strong> carry at
    least one citation. That number flatters the archive, and this page exists to say why.</p>

    <div class="note" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <strong>A family tree is not a source.</strong> Several of the most frequent citations below are
      other people's online trees — Geni, FamilySearch Family Tree, and named MyHeritage sites. Those
      record what somebody believed. They are shown because concealing them would be worse, but they
      carry no evidential weight in this archive, and a claim resting only on them is marked
      <span class="chip lore">family lore</span> or <span class="chip dis">disputed</span> however
      many trees repeat it. <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Record sets — these are evidence</h2>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li><strong>Queensland civil registration</strong> — births, deaths and marriages, from 1856.</li>
      <li><strong>New South Wales civil registration and parish registers</strong> — including
      St James's, Sydney, where George Lindsay D'Arcy was christened on 5 November 1829.</li>
      <li><strong>Australian newspapers (Trove)</strong> — death and funeral notices, and the
      Brisbane press.</li>
      <li><strong>National Archives of Australia, First World War service records</strong> — series
      B2455. The fourteen-page dossier of <em>Sergeant Lindesay Atkinson D'Arcy, 11335</em>, is known
      to exist and <strong>has not yet been read</strong>; a digitisation request is drafted.</li>
      <li><strong>Australian War Memorial</strong> — embarkation and nominal rolls, used here for
      <em>Sergeant Lindesay Atkinson D'Arcy, 11335</em> and <em>Private Arthur Hartley Sneyd, 5050</em>.
      The <strong>Roll of Honour circular</strong> for Sneyd — the form his family filled in by hand —
      is known to survive and <strong>has not yet been transcribed</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Brisbane City Council cemetery registers</strong> — Toowong, Dutton Park, South
      Brisbane, Nundah, Lutwyche, Mt Thompson.</li>
      <li><strong>Ireland, Births and Baptisms, 1620–1881</strong> (index, via FamilySearch) — the
      christening of Paul Cole Atkinson at Limerick on 9 July 1832, naming <em>Richard Atkinson</em>
      and <em>Anne</em>. <strong>Roots Ireland itself has not been searched</strong>; it needs a paid
      account, and a request is drafted.</li>
      <li><strong>Caribbean Birth &amp; Baptism Index 1590–1928</strong> — the christening of
      Constantine Darcy at St Michael, Barbados, 18 February 1786, naming <em>Robert Darcy</em> and
      <em>Jane</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Connolly and Edwards, <em>Roll of Officers of the Corps of Royal Engineers,
      1660–1898</em></strong> (R.E. Institute, Chatham, 1898) — entries 203 and 335, for Robert and
      Constantine D'Arcy. Searched in full via the Internet Archive scan.</li>
      <li><strong>Kent Burials</strong> and <strong>Hampshire Marriages</strong> (FindMyPast) — the
      burial of Robert D'Arcy at St Mary, Chatham, 19 May 1827, and his marriage to Jean Ward at
      Portsea St Mary, 21 June 1779.</li>
      <li><strong>Australia, Births and Baptisms, 1792–1981</strong> — the christening of George
      Lindsay Darcy at St James Sydney, 5 November 1829, naming both parents.</li>
      <li><strong>British Newspaper Archive, Family Notices</strong> — the marriage notice of
      "Captain George d'Arcy", son of "Lieut Colonel d'Arcy", <em>Salisbury and Winchester Journal</em>,
      23 September 1805.</li>
      <li><strong>Scotland, Civil Registration</strong> and <strong>Scotland, Marriages</strong>
      (indexes) — the birth of William Murdoch at St Quivox and Newton, 7 October 1856, naming Francis
      Murdoch and Marion Watt; and their marriage at Avondale, Lanarkshire, 6 May 1854.
      <strong>ScotlandsPeople itself, which holds the register images, has not been consulted</strong> —
      it needs a paid account, and a request is drafted.</li>
      <li><strong>Forebears</strong> — surname distribution, accessed 9 September 2026.
      <a href={u("/name")}>The name →</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Family documents held privately</h2>
    <ul class="dek">
      <li><strong>The eulogy for Kenneth Lindsay D'Arcy</strong>, March 2010 — four thousand words in
      three parts, delivered by Gayle, Ian and Graham D'Arcy. The single richest source in the
      archive. <a href={u("/documents")}>Read it →</a></li>
      <li><strong>A 1970 school photograph</strong>, Pimlico State High School, Townsville, showing
      Ian D'Arcy — which independently confirms the Townsville decade described in the eulogy.</li>
      <li><strong>The 'This Is Your Life' book</strong> presented to Ken D'Arcy on his retirement from
      National Mutual in 1982. Referred to in the eulogy; not yet located.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Every citation string in the tree, counted</h2>
    <p class="small">Exactly as carried in the exported GEDCOM, so that the balance between records
    and trees is visible rather than asserted.</p>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Citation</th><th class="m">People</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {ranked.map(([k, n]) => (<tr><td>{k}</td><td class="m">{n}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
